f 1050 960.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147695 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "8898632a-f568-581b-9193-aeb177e79e44" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148235 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186148236 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148235 #edb/ref 1061 13518 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148235 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "62e34955-26a4-56d7-b473-2f2540475511" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148235 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186148738 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148236 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186148237 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148236 #edb/ref 1061 13519 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148236 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "98d275b2-d6f2-53c5-b4d7-cae25b562f1a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148236 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186147397 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148237 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186148238 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148237 #edb/ref 1061 13520 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148237 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2755fa08-44ef-5ef5-b8c8-6470c68e446a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148237 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186148739 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148238 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186148239 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148238 #edb/ref 1061 13521 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148238 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "abe470e8-0b76-56c6-9c15-a1d10e391c4b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148238 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186147398 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148239 #edb/ref 1061 13522 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148239 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "5d3cad10-c38a-51fd-b011-83786859f767" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148239 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186147399 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148738 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose that a tank is filling up with water, and the rate at which water flows into the tank is given by\n\n$$\nr(t) = 600e^{-3t},\n$$\n\nwhere $r(t)$ is measured in liters per hour, and $t > 0$ is the time (in hours) since the water flow started. \n\nSuppose we want to know how much water enters the tank between $1$ and $2$ hours. \n\nLet's take note of the following:\n\n- We're interested in the amount of water that enters the tank between $t=1$ and $t=2.$ In other words, we want the **net change in volume** between these times. We're *not* finding the total volume of water in the tank.\n\n- We're given the **rate of change of volume** $r(t).$\n\nTo find the net change in volume, we integrate $r(t)$ between the two limits. This gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{2}600{e}^{-3t}\\,\\text{d}t & ={-\\frac{600}{3}{e}^{-3t}|}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & ={-200{e}^{-3t}|}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =-200({e}^{-6}-{e}^{-3}) \\\\  & =200({e}^{-3}-{e}^{-6}) \\\\  & =200{e}^{-6}({e}^{3}-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148738 #edb/ref 1065 2744 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148738 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "60a3bf2a-7142-575f-8381-dfb70827c7c2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148738 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148739 #edb/ref 1064 "If we're interested to find the **total number** or **total amount** of some quantity, we should use the fundamental theorem of calculus.\n\nLet's go back to the earlier example with the shopping mall (Example 1). Suppose that there are $2\\,000$ people inside the mall at the moment that it opened. How many people in total are inside the mall after $2$ hours?\n\nWe already know that the **rate of change** in the number of people is $R(t).$ Therefore, the **total number of people** in the mall at time $t$, which we'll call $T(t)$ is given by:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(t) & =T(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{t}R(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =T(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{t}r(x)-25\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Important**: You'll notice that we changed the variable of integration from $t$ to $x.$ This is to ensure that $T$ comes out as a function of $t,$ and also so we don't get confused. When we have a definite integral, it does not matter which variable we use!\n\nTo work out how many people are inside the mall at $t=2,$ we substitute $t=2$ into the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(2) & =T(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{2}r(x)-25\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\,000+{{{\\int }_{0}^{2}r(x)-25\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{850} \\\\  & =2\\,000+850 \\\\  & =2\\,850\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148739 #edb/ref 1065 2745 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148739 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "02950c6c-bf43-5c34-885c-8da2caa06fbf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148739 #edb/ref 1067 "Finding Total Amounts Given Rates of Change" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317591 #edb/ref 1080 "4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317591 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317591 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "06774482-8185-522a-b0da-abe466cad0be" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186318032 #edb/ref 1080 "188" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186318032 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186318032 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "8ae082c6-947e-5375-a9f4-aa88253e382d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186318510 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186318032 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186318510 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186318510 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186318510 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186318032 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186318510 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "2cc1a0df-9be5-533e-b00c-ee8fecf41b26" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186318615 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186317591 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186318615 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186318615 #edb/ref 1077 "field-2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186318615 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186317591 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186318615 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "57c75693-c5b3-5428-8192-d75e3b45beab" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186319359 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318510 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186319359 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186318615 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186319359 #edb/ref 1069 "q-349996" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186319359 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186319359 #edb/ref 1071 "On a certain afternoon, the total number of people $N(t)$ visiting the city's zoo is given by the function\n\n$$\nN(t)=60+{\\int }_{0}^{t}(48-3{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time (in hours) since noon.  \n\nAt what time is the number of people in the zoo at a maximum, and how many people are at the zoo at that time?\n\nThe maximum number of people is\n{{field-1}}\nafter\n{{field-2}}\nhours." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186319359 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8b0963b7-9111-575b-994d-33c581e812aa" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186319359 #edb/ref 1074 "To find when the number of people at the zoo is at maximum, we need to solve $\\frac{\\text{d}N}{\\text{d}t}=0.$ First, we differentiate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}N}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(60+{\\int }_{0}^{t}(48-3{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(60)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({\\int }_{0}^{t}(48-3{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{0}^{t}(48-3{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =48-3{t}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve $\\frac{\\text{d}N}{\\text{d}t}=0$:\n\n$$\n48-3{t}^{2}=0\\,⟹\\,t=4\n$$\n\nSo there are two candidates for global extrema, $t=0$ and $t=4.$ To find out how many people are in the zoo when $t=4,$ we figure out $N(4):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(4) & =60+{\\int }_{0}^{4}(48-3{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =60+{[48x-{x}^{3}]|}_{0}^{4} \\\\  & =60+[(48\\cdot 4-{4}^{3})-(0)] \\\\  & =60+[192-64] \\\\  & =60+128 \\\\  & =188\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $60<188$, we conclude that the maximum number of people at the zoo is $188$, and this will happen after $4$ hours." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186330238 #edb/ref 1080 "The minimum is $40$ participants  after $4$ days" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186330238 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186330238 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "21c10ece-c009-56c5-be8d-90e6cb75497c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186330645 #edb/ref 1080 "The minimum is $32$ participants  after $0$ days" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186330645 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186330645 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "4c7ffa2f-9e56-5efe-97c7-d12ccab36064" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186330698 #edb/ref 1080 "The minimum is $8$ participants  after $4$ days" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186330698 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186330698 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "522f2b59-ef0a-5794-8023-90cf98db584b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186331864 #edb/ref 1080 "The minimum is $40$ participants  after $0$ days" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186331864 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186331864 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "d7eb419f-421f-54a3-b0b8-0034e420363a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186332074 #edb/ref 1080 "The minimum is $32$ participants  after $4$ days" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186332074 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186332074 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "f18e8e04-35b4-55b7-86fa-527938e47ab2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186332638 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186330238 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186332638 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186330645 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186332638 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186330698 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186332638 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186331864 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186332638 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186332074 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186332638 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186332638 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186332638 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186330698 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186332638 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "f9450f60-3299-5ac4-9582-ce92545753e4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186332974 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186332638 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186332974 #edb/ref 1069 "q-62834" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186332974 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186332974 #edb/ref 1071 "The total number of participants $S(t)$ registered for an online conference after $t$ days is given by the function\n\n$$\nS(t)=40+{\\int }_{0}^{t}(4x-16)\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time, in days, since the conference was announced. \n\nAt what time is the number of participants at a minimum, and how many participants are registered at this time?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186332974 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "28fcc32f-48c3-5813-8388-8eda5e584ffe" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186332974 #edb/ref 1074 "To find when the number participants is minimized, we need to solve $\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t}=0.$ First, we differentiate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(40+{\\int }_{0}^{t}(4x-16)\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(40)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({\\int }_{0}^{t}(4x-16)\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{0}^{t}(4x-16)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4t-16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve $\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t}=0$:\n\n$$\n4x-16=0\\,⟹\\,t=4\n$$\n\nSo there are two candidates for global extrema, $t=0$ and $t=4.$ To find out how many participants are registered when $t=4,$ we figure out $S(4)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(4) & =40+{\\int }_{0}^{4}(4x-16)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =40+{[2{x}^{2}-16x]|}_{0}^{4} \\\\  & =40+[(2(4)^{2}-16(4))-(0)] \\\\  & =40-32 \\\\  & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $8<40$, we conclude that the minimum number of participants is $8$, and this occurs after $4$ days." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505912 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186506613 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505912 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186506631 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505912 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186506642 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505