17592186045417] [17592186506397 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186506184 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186506397 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186506397 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186506397 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186506184 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186506397 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "265736ea-5f99-5ebe-9b6c-32c27324902a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186506451 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186506247 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186506451 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186506451 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186506451 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186506247 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186506451 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "9eaf115f-1435-52c2-b951-5e9b2f69cb63" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186506469 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186506332 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186506469 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186506469 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186506469 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186506332 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186506469 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "bdb50e03-36f7-5d1f-9178-33250a489690" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186506541 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186506396 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186506541 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186506397 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186506541 #edb/ref 1069 "q-349997" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186506541 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186506541 #edb/ref 1071 "On a certain afternoon, the total number of people $N(t)$ visiting the city's zoo is given by the function\n\n$$\nN(t)=30+4{\\int }_{0}^{t}(10-2x)\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time (in hours) since noon.  \n\nAt what time is the number of people in the zoo at a maximum, and how many people are at the zoo at that time?\n\nThe maximum number of people is\n{{field-1}}\nafter\n{{field-2}}\nhours." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186506541 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "54f0409c-f2ef-5cc4-b4e4-ffc38eb896a2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186506541 #edb/ref 1074 "To find when the number of people at the zoo is at maximum, we need to solve $\\frac{\\text{d}N}{\\text{d}t}=0.$ First, we differentiate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}N}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(30+4{\\int }_{0}^{t}(10-2x)\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(30)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4{\\int }_{0}^{t}(10-2x)\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =4\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{0}^{t}(10-2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4(10-2t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve $\\frac{\\text{d}N}{\\text{d}t}=0$:\n\n$$\n4(10-2t)=0\\,⟹\\,t=5\n$$\n\nSo there are two candidates for global extrema, $t=0$ and $t=5.$ To find out how many people are in the zoo when $t=5,$ we figure out $N(5):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(5) & =30+4{\\int }_{0}^{5}(10-2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =30+4{[10x-{x}^{2}]|}_{0}^{5} \\\\  & =30+4[(10\\cdot 5-{5}^{2})-(0)] \\\\  & =30+4\\cdot 25 \\\\  & =130\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $30<130$, we conclude that the maximum number of people at the zoo is $130$, and this will happen after $5$ hours." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186506554 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186506451 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186506554 #edb/ref 1069 "q-349984" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186506554 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186506554 #edb/ref 1071 "A city is tracking the number of bikes available in a bike-share station.\nThey observe that bikes are returned to the station at a rate $r(t)$ given by the function\n\n$$\nr(t)=5{t}^{3}+2t,\n$$\n\nwhere $r(t)$ is measured in bikes per hour, and $t>0$ is the time in hours since tracking began.\nAt the same time, $4$ bikes are rented from the station each hour.\n\nIf there were initially $100$ bikes at the station, find the expression that gives $B(t),$ the number of bikes at the station $t$ hours after tracking began.\n\n$B(t)=\\frac{5}{4}{t}^{4}\\,+$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186506554 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7896667b-b339-512f-9dbb-66fd90855442" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186506554 #edb/ref 1074 "The number of bikes is increasing at a rate of $r(t)$ bikes per hour and is decreasing at a rate of $4$ bikes per hour. Therefore, the **rate of change** $R(t)$ of the number of bikes is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(t) & =r(t)-4 \\\\  & =5{t}^{3}+2t-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the total number $B(t)$ of bikes after $t$ hours, we apply the second fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B(t) & =B(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{t}R(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =100+{\\int }_{0}^{t}(5{x}^{3}+2x-4)\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo ensure that our final function is a function of $t$ only, we use a different variable ($x$) for the integration.\n\nWe can simplify the expression as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B(t) & =100+{\\int }_{0}^{t}5{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{t}2x\\,\\text{d}x-{\\int }_{0}^{t}4\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =100+{5\\,\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}|}_{0}^{t}+{2\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}|}_{0}^{t}-4x{|}_{0}^{t} \\\\  & =100+\\frac{5}{4}{t}^{4}+{t}^{2}-4t \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}{t}^{4}+\\,{t}^{2}-4t+100\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186506580 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186506469 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186506580 #edb/ref 1069 "q-349973" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186506580 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186506580 #edb/ref 1071 "The rate at which water flows into a tank is given by the function\n\n$$\nr(t)=6t+10,\n$$\n\nwhere $r(t)$ is measured in liters per hour and $t>0$ is the time in hours since $1\\,\\text{p.m}.$\nWater is also draining from the tank at a steady rate of $8$ liters per hour.\n\nIf there were $200$ liters of water in the tank at $1\\,\\text{p.m},$ how many liters of water will be there at $5\\,\\text{p.m}?$\n\nThere will be \n{{field-1}}  liters of water in the tank at $5\\,\\text{p.m}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186506580 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b1c05acb-14b2-5a23-a0fb-57fef367611e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186506580 #edb/ref 1074 "Water is flowing into the tank at a rate of $6t+10$ liters per hour and is draining at a rate of $8$ liters per hour. Therefore, the **rate of change** in the amount of water (call it $R(t)$) is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(t) & =r(t)-8 \\\\  & =6t+10-8 \\\\  & =6t+2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the **total amount of water** after $t$ hours (call it $T(t)$), we apply the second fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(t) & =T(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{t}R(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =200+{\\int }_{0}^{t}(6x+2)\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo ensure that $T$ is a function of $t$ only, we use a different variable ($x$) for the integration. \n\nTo find out the amount of water after $4$ hours ($5\\,\\text{p.m}$), we substitute $t=4$ into the above and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(4) & =200+{\\int }_{0}^{4}(6x+2)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =200+(3{x}^{2}+2x){|}_{0}^{4} \\\\  & =200+[(3\\cdot 16+8)-0] \\\\  & =200+56 \\\\  & =256\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, there will be $256$ liters of water in the tank at $5\\,\\text{p.m}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186506613 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186506651 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186506613 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "25fcc6e6-29c0-5bf9-9f04-6426e5b05635" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186506613 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186319359 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186506631 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186506642 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186506631 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "6c504d06-98a0-51e0-a67b-97bcf359f7c7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186506631 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186332974 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186506642 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186506655 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186506642 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "a55587c2-e7e9-5369-8800-e5f86ed901d2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186506642 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186506580 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186506651 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c49eac7d-056b-55ba-a0ba-a1b3b42a5b43" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186506651 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186506541 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186506655 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186506613 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186506655 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "d54dc8bc-ee1a-54b4-8dbf-4d22b96df654" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186506655 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186506554 #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
